      title: "The Prophet in Mecca",
      body: `## Mecca and Quraysh

**Mecca** lay in a barren valley of the Hijaz, the mountainous region along Arabia's Red Sea coast. It had no agriculture to speak of. What it had was a sanctuary, the **Ka'ba**, a cube-shaped shrine around which the tribes of the region came on pilgrimage, and a truce of the sacred months during which fighting was forbidden and markets could be held. The tradition says the Ka'ba housed some 360 idols, among them Hubal, and that the great goddesses al-Lat, al-'Uzza, and Manat were honored nearby. Above all these stood a high god, **Allah**—the word simply means "the God."

Mecca was ruled by the tribe of **Quraysh**, divided into clans. They kept the sanctuary, profited from the pilgrimage, and—according to the tradition—sent caravans to Syria and Yemen. How great this trade was is one of the questions Crone raised; it may have been more modest than the sources suggest, dealing in leather and other local goods rather than spices. Either way, Quraysh were the leading tribe of western Arabia, and their wealth and pride are a constant theme of the early suras.

## An orphan of Hashim

Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah was born in Mecca into the clan of **Hashim**, an honored but not especially rich branch of Quraysh. The tradition dates his birth to the "Year of the Elephant," when an Ethiopian army from Yemen, under its general Abraha, marched on Mecca with war elephants and was turned back; this is usually reckoned as about 570, though the expedition may have taken place some years earlier. His father died before he was born, his mother when he was six, and his grandfather, 'Abd al-Muttalib, two years later. He was raised by his uncle **Abu Talib**, head of the clan.

The tradition remembers him as a trusted young man, nicknamed *al-Amin*, "the trustworthy," who worked in the caravan trade. Around the age of twenty-five he entered the service of a wealthy widow, **Khadija**, who managed her own trading interests; impressed by his honesty, she proposed marriage. The tradition says she was forty, but she bore him several children, which makes her age doubtful. Of their children, four daughters survived to adulthood, among them **Fatima**, who would marry Muhammad's cousin 'Ali. For some twenty-five years Muhammad was Khadija's only husband.

## "Recite!"

Muhammad was given, the sources say, to retreats in a cave on **Mount Hira**, outside the city, where he prayed and fasted. Around 610, in the month of Ramadan, he had an experience he at first did not understand. In the account preserved by Ibn Ishaq and al-Bukhari, an angel—later identified as **Gabriel**—seized him and commanded him to recite. The first words revealed, by most accounts, are those that open sura 96:

> Read: In the name of thy Lord who createth,
> Createth man from a clot.
> Read: And it is thy Lord the Most Bountiful,
> Who teacheth by the pen,
> Teacheth man that which he knew not.
>
> — Qur'an 96:1–5, trans. Marmaduke Pickthall (1930)

Terrified, Muhammad went home to Khadija, who comforted him and took him to her cousin Waraqa ibn Nawfal, a Christian, who is said to have recognized the experience as prophecy like that of Moses. Khadija is remembered as the first to believe. She was followed by Muhammad's young cousin **'Ali**, his freed slave and adopted son **Zayd ibn Haritha**, and his friend **Abu Bakr**, a merchant who would become the first caliph.

## The early message

The suras that tradition and modern scholars place in the Meccan period are short, rhythmic, and urgent. Their themes are few and powerful.

- **One God.** God is the creator of all things, who alone deserves worship. The idols can neither help nor harm. The short sura 112 sums it up: "Say: He is Allah, the One! Allah, the eternally Besought of all! He begetteth not nor was begotten. And there is none comparable unto Him."
- **The Judgment.** The world will end. The dead will be raised, and every soul will see what it has done—"whoso doeth good an atom's weight will see it then, and whoso doeth ill an atom's weight will see it then" (99:7–8). Paradise and hell are described in vivid images.
- **Gratitude and justice.** The signs of God are everywhere in nature, and human beings owe him thanks. That thanks is expressed in prayer and in care for the weak: the orphan, the beggar, the traveler. Sura 107 condemns the man "who repelleth the orphan, and urgeth not the feeding of the needy."
- **The prophets.** God has sent messengers before—Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Arabian prophets such as Hud and Salih—and peoples who rejected them were destroyed.

The Qur'an calls Muhammad a "warner" and a "messenger" (*rasul*). It insists that he is a man, not a miracle-worker, and that the Qur'an itself is the sign of his prophecy.

## Rejection

For a few years Muhammad preached quietly. When, around 613, he began to preach in public, the leaders of Quraysh turned against him. The message threatened them on several fronts: it condemned the gods of their fathers, mocked their wealth, and—if the pilgrimage depended on the idols—threatened their livelihood. Above all, a prophet claiming God's authority was a rival to their leadership.

Muhammad himself was protected by his clan, for to harm him would have meant blood feud with Hashim, and Abu Talib, though he never became a Muslim, refused to give him up. Converts without such protection—slaves and the poor—were mistreated. The tradition remembers **Bilal**, an Ethiopian slave, forced to lie under a heavy stone in the sun, crying "One! One!" until Abu Bakr bought his freedom. Around 615 a group of believers emigrated across the Red Sea to the Christian kingdom of **Aksum** in Ethiopia, whose ruler, the Negus, gave them refuge.

Quraysh then boycotted the whole clan of Hashim, refusing to trade or intermarry with them, for perhaps two or three years. The boycott ended, but in 619—remembered as the "Year of Sorrow"—both Khadija and Abu Talib died. The new head of Hashim, another uncle, Abu Lahab, was hostile. Muhammad's protection in Mecca was at an end.

> **Pause and consider.** Why might the message of the early suras have seemed so threatening to the leaders of Quraysh? Was the conflict mainly religious, social, or political—and can those be separated in a place like Mecca?`,
